{"id":"9810d72bd83ec520dedd5eafcd3a91a79075ade0e29e2601e98f6e9b596b1ff7","lamport":0,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-04T12:18:03.194446Z","format_version":1,"kind":"create","payload":{"fields":{"created-at":"2026-10-04T12:18:03.194446Z","created-by":"carlo.cifarelli@gmail.com","description":"Step 5/6 of the op-log roadmap (parent 0e7b1245).\n\nPer-entity identity chain with a public key (linear version chain, fast-forward\nmerge). Sign operations for verified authorship across clones. Builds on the\nop-log flagship.","parent":"0e7b1245-e860-4843-9df3-101cd9a0a746","priority":"4","spec":"# Spec — Identity + signed ops (verified authorship)\n\nRoadmap step 5/6 (parent 0e7b12). Depends on step 4 (op-log). Medium effort.\n\n## Goal\n\nAttach a verifiable identity to each operation so authorship survives across\nclones and cannot be spoofed, mirroring git-bug's identity subsystem.\n\n## Current state\n\n- Authorship today is just `self.session.email()` written into `created-by` /\n  comment `author` — a bare, unverifiable string.\n- Ops (step 4) already carry an `author` field; this step makes it trustworthy.\n\n## Design\n\n- Identity as a linear version chain (git-bug precedent): an append-only sequence\n  of identity ops under a dedicated namespace, each version carrying a public key;\n  fast-forward merge semantics (no branching identities).\n- Proposed key layout: `ticgit:identities:<identity-uuid>:versions:<n>` with the\n  current public key derivable by folding the chain. (Finalize alongside step 4's\n  key helpers in `keys.rs`.)\n- Sign each operation's content-id (step 1) with the author's private key; store\n  the signature in the op envelope. Verify on replay; an op with a bad/missing\n  signature is surfaced by `ti verify` and excluded from trusted state (but, per\n  step 3, retained in the log).\n- Key management: reuse the user's existing git/SSH or GPG key where possible to\n  avoid a bespoke keystore; private keys never enter git-meta.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `oplog` envelope gains `signature` + `identity` reference.\n- New `identity` module; `ti user` commands extended to create/show an identity\n  with a public key (today `ti users` is only a nick↔email mailmap — this is a\n  superset, keep the mailmap working).\n- `ti verify` extended to check signatures.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Unit/CLI test: an op signed by identity A verifies; a tampered op fails\n  verification and is reported by `ti verify`.\n- Backward-compat: unsigned legacy ops (pre-identity) still replay (trusted-by-\n  default or flagged, per partner decision).\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Keystore: reuse SSH/GPG vs ship our own — decide before coding.\n- Trust model for unsigned/legacy ops.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Key management UX is the hard part; crypto itself is straightforward. Deferred\n  behind the flagship deliberately so the distributed-tracker story ships first.\n\n## Decisions locked 2026-10-04\n\n- KEYSTORE: reuse the user's existing SSH key (ed25519), git-signing style. No\n  bespoke keystore; private key never leaves ~/.ssh / the agent. The public key\n  lives in the synced identity chain for cross-clone verification.\n- TRUST MODEL: unsigned ops (legacy, or from clients without an identity) are\n  TRUSTED BY DEFAULT and contribute to state; `ti verify` flags them as\n  `unsigned` (warning, not error). Soft transition — existing repos keep working\n  and signing is adopted gradually.","state":"review","status":"open","title":"Identity + signed ops (verified authorship)"}}}